## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to orchestrate host-level TLS termination with per-tenant dynamic routing, zero-downtime reloads, and proper security boundaries using Podman, systemd, SELinux, and nftables. It evaluates practical skills across containerization, reverse proxy configuration, and kernel policy tooling.

## Key Concepts
- Podman containerization with bind mounts for certificates and mappings
- SNI-based routing with per-tenant map files
- Live configuration reloads via nginx signal handling or systemd ExecReload
- Minimal SELinux policy to permit TLS termination and certificate reading
- nftables inbound rules for port 8443 and outbound rules for backend communication

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watcher to rebuild nginx.conf from /srv/mappings
const fs = require('fs');
const { execSync } = require('child_process');

const mappingsPath = '/srv/mappings';
const template = 
`events { worker_connections 1024; }
http {
  map $ssl_server_name $backend {
    include /srv/mappings/*.map;
  }
  server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /srv/certs/$ssl_server_name.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /srv/certs/$ssl_server_name.key;
    location / {
      proxy_pass http://$backend;
    }
  }
}`;

function reloadNginx() {
  fs.writeFileSync('/etc/nginx/nginx.conf', template);
  execSync('nginx -s reload', { stdio: 'inherit' });
}

fs.watch(mappingsPath, (eventType, filename) => {
  if (filename && filename.endsWith('.map')) {
    console.log(`Mapping changed: ${filename}`);
    reloadNginx();
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests practical container containment, SELinux policy implementation, audit trail configuration, and host isolation in a realistic data ingestion workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Podman user namespaces for UID/GID isolation
- SELinux targeted policy modules for domain confinement
- Auditd path monitoring for comprehensive file I/O tracking
- Secure bind mounts with minimal host filesystem exposure

## Code Example
```bash
# Create SELinux module
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

# Policy rules for ingest_t domain
allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
```

## Implementation Commands
```bash
# Create dedicated user
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/false ingest
sudo usermod -v 100000-199999 -w 100000-199999 ingest

# Build and load SELinux module
cat > ingest.te << 'EOF'
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
EOF

checkmodule -M -m -o ingest.mod ingest.te
semodule_package -o ingest.pp -m ingest.mod
sudo semodule -i ingest.pp

# Configure audit rules
sudo auditctl -w /opt/ingest/config -p r -k ingest_config
sudo auditctl -w /srv/ingest/data -p rwxa -k ingest_data
sudo auditctl -w /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock -p w -k ingest_ipc

# Run container
podman run \
  --uidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  --gidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  -v /opt/ingest/config:/opt/ingest/config:ro \
  -v /srv/ingest/data:/srv/ingest/data:rw \
  -v /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock:/var/run/ingest-proxy.sock \
  --network=none \
  --security-opt label=type:ingest_t \
  --user 1000 \
  ingest-service:latest
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explore dynamic per-tenant TLS policy reloads and zero-downtime upgrades in a real RHCSA context.

## Key Concepts
- TLS per-tenant policies, SNI, Redis-backed config, hot-reload, Podman, systemd drop-ins, SELinux, nftables

## Code Example
```nginx
# sample
http {
  include /etc/nginx/tenant_policies.conf;
  ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
  map $tls_sni $tenant { default back; };
}
```

